ier to gauge the size of the planet  and the atmosphere around it using the  transit method  timing variations a planet that is  orbiting a star can create variations of  timing for orbiting periods of other  planets that orbit the same star  this can confirm the presence of one or  more planets but doesn't give us much  other information about them  gravitational lensing this technique is  used if there is a star in front of  another star  and the gravitational field of the star  in front creates a lens  and magnifies the light from the star  behind it and any planets orbiting that  star  this method is useful for finding  planets around stars in the center of  our galaxy  where there are a multitude of  background stars to create the lensing  effect  direct imaging this is a very difficult  way to discover exoplanets  planets reflect light from the star that  they orbit when they are close to the  star they are lost in the glare  when they are far away from the star  they do not reflect much light  if the planet is very large larger than  jupiter it can give off a significant  infrared signature that makes it more  visible  astronomers can use a coronagraph to  block out the light from the star  and make it more visible as well the  kepler mission was designed by the  united states and nasa to look at a very  small portion of our part of the milky  way galaxy  to find exoplanets and specifically to  find earth-sized exoplanets that orbit  the habitable region of the parent star  and then to extrapolate that information  to determine how many billions of stars  in our milky way galaxy  have these potentially habitable  earth-sized planets  as of january 1st 2014 the kepler  mission has found  3568 candidate planets  1055 are confirmed and reside in 801  planetary systems most of the planets  discovered are jupiter size or larger  and consist mostly of gas a small  percentage  one plus percent are earth size and our  rocky inconsistence  there is a larger percentage